<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">13 December in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 13 December, from 1076 to 1939.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1076">1076 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">950 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Salerno surrenders to the Norman Robert Guiscard</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a>, the Norman conqueror who had married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichelgaita">Sikelgaita</a>, daughter of Guaimar IV, took Salerno from his brother-in-law Gisulf II, its last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> prince. Gisulf held out in the citadel until May of the following year.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1294">1294 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">732 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pope Celestine V resigns after five months</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The hermit-pope Celestine V renounced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, overwhelmed by its politics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> appears to condemn him for 'the great refusal', but the church later canonised him.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1545">1545 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">481 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Council of Trent opens</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Catholic Church's council opened at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and met, with long breaks, until 1563. It reaffirmed Catholic doctrine against Protestant teaching and reformed clerical discipline.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1577">1577 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">449 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Francis Drake sets sail from Plymouth to circle the world</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a> left Plymouth with five ships on an expedition authorised by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>. Called a voyage of discovery but in effect a covert raiding voyage, it returned in September 1580 as the second circumnavigation of the world.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1642">1642 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">384 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Abel Tasman sights New Zealand</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> navigator Abel Tasman sighted the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s South Island, the first recorded European sighting. A violent encounter with Māori in Golden Bay days later meant his crew never landed.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1643">1643 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">383 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Sir William Waller surprises a Royalist garrison at Alton</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Parliamentarian forces under Sir William Waller surprised a winter garrison of Royalist infantry and cavalry under the Earl of Crawford at Alton in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>. It was the first decisive defeat of Sir Ralph Hopton, the Royalist leader in the south.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-english-civil-war">First English Civil War</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1937">1937 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">89 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Japanese troops capture Nanjing</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Japanese forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, and began weeks of mass killing, rape and looting. Estimates of the dead range from tens of thousands to over 300,000, and the massacre remains bitterly contested.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1939">1939 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">87 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">British cruisers fight the Graf Spee at the River Plate</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Three British cruisers engaged the German pocket battleship Admiral Graf Spee off the River Plate estuary. Damaged, she took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>, and four days later her captain scuttled her.</p></div></li></ol>
